"prenatal","age_last":"5 years","phenotypes":[{"text":"prenatal femoral bowing","hpo_label":"Femoral bowing","hpo_id":"HP:0002980","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"femoral fractures at birth","hpo_label":"Femur fracture","hpo_id":"HP:0031846","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"recurrent fractures","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"dentinogenesis imperfecta","hpo_label":"Dentinogenesis imperfecta","hpo_id":"HP:0000703","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"normal mentality","hpo_label":"Intellectual disability","hpo_id":"HP:0001249","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"generalized bone deformities","hpo_label":"Abnormality of the skeletal system","hpo_id":"HP:0000924","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"joint hyperextensibility","hpo_label":"Joint hypermobility","hpo_id":"HP:0001382","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"arachnodactyly","hpo_label":"Arachnodactyly","hpo_id":"HP:0001166","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"large umbilical hernia","hpo_label":"Umbilical hernia","hpo_id":"HP:0001537","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"wormian bones","hpo_label":"Wormian bones","hpo_id":"HP:0002645","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"scoliosis","hpo_label":"Scoliosis","hpo_id":"HP:0002650","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"platyspondyly","hpo_label":"Platyspondyly","hpo_id":"HP:0000926","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"wide symphysis pubis","hpo_label":"Wide pubic symphysis","hpo_id":"HP:0003183","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"short stature (-7.2 SD)","hpo_label":"Severe short stature","hpo_id":"HP:0003510","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"osteoporosis","hpo_label":"Osteoporosis","hpo_id":"HP:0000939","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"slightly elevated alkaline phosphatase","hpo_label":"Elevated circulating alkaline phosphatase concentration","hpo_id":"HP:0003155","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"hearing loss","hpo_label":"Hearing impairment","hpo_id":"HP:0000365","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.747C>G (p.Phe249Leu)","cdna":"c.747C>G","protein":"p.Phe249Leu","genomic":"","zygosity":"homozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"missense","key":"V1"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"Both affected sibs homozygous; parents and two normal sibs heterozygous","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["Patient II-6 is the 5-year-old younger brother of patient II-3. Bilateral femoral bowing was detected intrauterine by fetal ultra-sound and fracture of both femora was noted at birth.","X-ray survey revealed wormian bones of skull, deformed long bones with lack of modeling, fractures and callus formation, scoliosis, platyspondyly, and wide symphyses pubis (Fig. 1B and H).","Bone densitometry (DEXA) at 5 years revealed borderline osteoporosis at the spine (Z-score −1.63) and osteoporosis at the left femur (Z-score −2.75)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:22482805:1","pmid":"22482805","label":"Consanguineous family with BMP1 c.34G>C","kind":"family","family":"","n_affected":null,"n_families":1,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"yes","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"increased bone mineral density","hpo_label":"Increased bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0011001","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"multiple recurrent fractures","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.34G>C","cdna":"c.34G>C","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"homozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"missense","key":"V2"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["we found a homozygous causative BMP1 mutation, c.34G>C, in a consanguineous family affected by increased bone mineral density and multiple recurrent fractures"],"quote_grounding":[1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:24648371:1","pmid":"24648371","label":"Newly identified patient","kind":"individual","family":"","n_affected":1,"n_families":1,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"Osteogenesis imperfecta","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"Normal bone density","hpo_label":"Increased bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0011001","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.34G>C","cdna":"c.34G>C","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"homozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"","key":"V2"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["we describe an additional patient with osteogenesis imperfecta with normal bone density and a recurrent, homozygous c.34G>C mutation in BMP1"],"quote_grounding":[1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:25214535:1","pmid":"25214535","label":"Three unrelated individuals homozygous for c.*241T>C","kind":"group","family":"","n_affected":3,"n_families":3,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"childhood","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"lower extremity fractures","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"elevated volumetric cortical bone mineral density","hpo_label":"Increased bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0011001","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"delayed onset of mineralization at bone formation sites","hpo_label":"Abnormality of bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0004348","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"hypermineralized bone matrix","hpo_label":"Abnormality of bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0004348","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"impaired procollagen type I C-propeptide cleavage in fibroblasts","hpo_label":"","hpo_id":"","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.*241T>C","cdna":"c.*241T>C","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"homozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"3'UTR polyadenylation signal","key":"V3"}],"variant_roles":["causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["Three individuals were homozygous for a nucleotide change in BMP1, affecting the polyadenylation signal of the transcript that codes for the short isoform of BMP1 (BMP1-1) (c.*241T>C). In skin fibroblasts of these individuals, we found low levels of BMP1-1 transcript and protein.","Peripheral quantitative computed tomography showed that all individuals had elevated volumetric cortical bone mineral density.","indicated that the onset of mineralization at bone formation sites was delayed, but that mineralized matrix was hypermineralized"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:25214535:2","pmid":"25214535","label":"Fourth individual, compound heterozygous","kind":"individual","family":"","n_affected":1,"n_families":1,"sex":"","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"childhood","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"lower extremity fractures","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"elevated volumetric cortical bone mineral density","hpo_label":"Increased bone mineral density","hpo_id":"HP:0011001","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"impaired procollagen type I C-propeptide cleavage in fibroblasts","hpo_label":"","hpo_id":"","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.*241T>C","cdna":"c.*241T>C","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"compound_heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"3'UTR polyadenylation signal","key":"V3"},{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.2107G>C","cdna":"c.2107G>C","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"compound_heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"splicing","key":"V4"}],"variant_roles":["causal","causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["The fourth individual was compound heterozygous for the c.*241T>C variant in BMP1-1 and a variant in BMP1 exon 15 (c.2107G>C) that affected splicing in both BMP1-1 and the long isoform of BMP1 (BMP1-3).","Both the homozygous 3'UTR variant and the compound heterozygous variants were associated with impaired procollagen type I C-propeptide cleavage"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:25402547:1","pmid":"25402547","label":"Korean OI patient","kind":"individual","family":"","n_affected":1,"n_families":1,"sex":"","ancestry":"Korean","consanguinity":"not_stated","onset":"","age_last":"","phenotypes":[{"text":"umbilical hernia","hpo_label":"Umbilical hernia","hpo_id":"HP:0001537","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"frequent fractures","hpo_label":"Recurrent fractures","hpo_id":"HP:0002757","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"markedly short stature","hpo_label":"Severe short stature","hpo_id":"HP:0003510","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"delayed motor development","hpo_label":"Delayed gross motor development","hpo_id":"HP:0002194","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"scoliosis","hpo_label":"Scoliosis","hpo_id":"HP:0002650","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"radial head dislocation","hpo_label":"Dislocated radial head","hpo_id":"HP:0003083","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"bowed radius","hpo_label":"Radial bowing","hpo_id":"HP:0002986","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"bowed ulna","hpo_label":"Ulnar bowing","hpo_id":"HP:0003031","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.808A>G p.(Met270Val)","cdna":"c.808A>G","protein":"p.(Met270Val)","genomic":"","zygosity":"compound_heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"missense","key":"V5"},{"gene":"BMP1","as_reported":"c.1297G>T","cdna":"c.1297G>T","protein":"","genomic":"","zygosity":"compound_heterozygous","origin":"unknown","consequence":"splice (exon 10 skipping)","key":"V6"}],"variant_roles":["causal","causal"],"segregation":"","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["We applied whole-exome sequencing to identify mutations in a Korean OI patient who had an umbilical hernia, frequent fractures, a markedly short stature, delayed motor development, scoliosis, and dislocation of the radial head, with a bowed radius and ulna.","We identified two novel variants in the BMP1 gene: c.808A>G and c.1297G>T. The former variant caused a missense change p.(Met270Val) and the latter variant caused the skipping of exon 10."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:25402547:1","pmid":"25402547","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Cho et al. (2015) showed in a zebrafish assay that both BMP1 variants found in a Korean OI patient, the missense p.(Met270Val) and c.1297G>T, which caused skipping of exon 10, were hypofunctional.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The hypofunctional nature of the two variants was demonstrated in a zebrafish assay."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:25214535:1","pmid":"25214535","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Fahiminiya et al. (2015) performed whole-exome sequencing in 4 unrelated children with a bone fragility disorder characterized by lower extremity fractures and a bone tissue mineralization defect, and identified a BMP1 polyadenylation signal variant (c.*241T>C) affecting the short isoform BMP1-1, homozygous in 3 and compound heterozygous with a splicing variant (c.2107G>C) in 1. They concluded that isolated lack of BMP1-1 causes bone fragility in children.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["We have now performed whole-exome sequencing on four unrelated individuals with this phenotype.","These results show that isolated lack of BMP1-1 causes bone fragility in children."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25214535:2","pmid":"25214535","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Fahiminiya et al. (2015) found low BMP1-1 transcript and protein levels in skin fibroblasts of homozygous patients, and reduced free procollagen type I C-propeptide in fibroblast supernatants of both homozygous and compound heterozygous patients, indicating impaired C-propeptide cleavage.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["In skin fibroblasts of these individuals, we found low levels of BMP1-1 transcript and protein.","as the amount of free C-propeptide in the supernatant of skin fibroblasts was less than in controls"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:25214535:3","pmid":"25214535","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Fahiminiya et al. (2015) noted that the disorder featured a mineralization defect in bone tissue but not at the growth plate; iliac bone histomorphometry and quantitative backscattered electron imaging showed delayed onset of mineralization at bone formation sites but hypermineralized matrix, and pQCT showed elevated cortical volumetric BMD.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["characterized mainly by lower extremity fractures and a mineralization defect in bone tissue but not on the growth plate level","indicated that the onset of mineralization at bone formation sites was delayed, but that mineralized matrix was hypermineralized"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:24648371:1","pmid":"24648371","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Valencia et al. (2014) found that dermal fibroblasts from a patient with OI and homozygous BMP1 c.34G>C mutation showed decreased protein levels of both alternatively spliced BMP1 products and abnormal cleavage of the C-terminal propeptide of type I procollagen.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Western blot analysis of dermal fibroblasts from this patient showed decreased protein levels of the two alternatively spliced products of BMP1 and abnormal cleavage of the C-terminal propeptide of type I procollagen."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:24648371:2","pmid":"24648371","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Valencia et al. (2014) showed by fluorescence and electron microscopy impaired assembly of type I collagen fibrils in the extracellular matrix of cultured fibroblasts from their patient and from a previously reported patient with a homozygous BMP1 c.747C>G mutation, concluding that BMP1 is essential for human type I collagen fibrillogenesis.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["fluorescence and electron microscopy showed impaired assembly of type I collagen fibrils in the extracellular matrix of cultured fibroblasts derived from two patients: the patient described here and a previously reported patient with a homozygous BMP1 c.747C>G mutation","We conclude that BMP1 is essential for human type I collagen fibrilogenesis."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22482805:1","pmid":"22482805","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Asharani et al. (2012) identified the homozygous BMP1 c.34G>C mutation by whole-exome sequencing combined with filtering for homozygous stretches. The mutation lies within the BMP1 signal peptide and led to impaired secretion and altered posttranslational modification.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Combining whole-exome sequencing and filtering for homozygous stretches of identified variants","The mutation is located within the BMP1 signal peptide and leads to impaired secretion and an alteration in posttranslational modification."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22482805:2","pmid":"22482805","section":"animal_model","statement":"Asharani et al. (2012) characterized a zebrafish bone mutant with lesions in bmp1a, showing that BMP1 function in osteogenesis is conserved across species. Genetic, biochemical, and histological analyses showed that Bmp1a is critically required to generate mature collagen in bone, downstream of osteoblast maturation.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["We also characterize a zebrafish bone mutant harboring lesions in bmp1a, demonstrating conservation of BMP1 function in osteogenesis across species.","reveal that Bmp1a is critically required for mature-collagen generation, downstream of osteoblast maturation, in bone"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22052668:1","pmid":"22052668","section":"mapping","statement":"By homozygosity mapping in a consanguineous Egyptian family with AR-OI, Martínez-Glez et al. (2012) excluded known AR-OI loci and identified 2 shared homozygous regions, including a 10.27-Mb region on chromosome 8p containing 84 genes, among them BMP1.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Taking an arbitrary cutoff of 1 MB of consecutive homozygous SNPs, we identified two large homozygous DNA regions on chromosomes 3 and 8p, which were present in the patients but absent in the other two normal siblings.","The 8p region consisted of 10.27 MB and contained 84 genes."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22052668:2","pmid":"22052668","section":"population_genetics","statement":"Martínez-Glez et al. (2012) did not find the BMP1 c.747C>G mutation in 100 control chromosomes of the same ethnic origin or in 884 European control chromosomes.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["The mutation was not found in 100 control chromosomes of the same ethnic origin or in 884 normal chromosomes from controls of European ethnicity."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:22052668:3","pmid":"22052668","section":"pathogenesis","statement":"Martínez-Glez et al. (2012) found decreased pN-alpha-1(I) relative to pC-alpha-1(I) and reduced free C-propeptide in supernatants of patient fibroblasts, indicating diminished procollagen I C-propeptidase activity, though residual activity remained; heterozygous cells were slightly impaired. Collagen overmodification was absent and BMP1/mTLD protein levels were unchanged.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Compared to control cells, LF-68 analysis of supernatants from patient-derived cells demonstrated a significant decrease in the amount of pNα1(I) with respect to pCα1(I) in both patients, thus indicating diminished PICP-peptidase activity in homozygous mutant cells","Pepsin-treated procollagens purified from the medium and cell layers of both patient and heterozygous fibroblasts showed a normal migration patterns of collagen type I, III, and V, indicating lack of collagen overmodification"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22052668:4","pmid":"22052668","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Martínez-Glez et al. (2012) showed that overexpression of wildtype mTLD in NIH3T3 fibroblasts led to C-terminal processing of most secreted proalpha-1(I), whereas the Phe249Leu mutant did not enhance processing; only wildtype mTLD rescued the processing defect in patient fibroblasts.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["In contrast, the supernatants of cells overexpressing mutant mTLD were found with similar levels of proα1(I) C-terminal processing as the control cells transduced with the empty retroviral vector, thus indicating that the Phe249Leu mutation causes strong reduction of the BMP1/mTLD proteolytic function","found the same result with only expression of the wild-type protein being able to rescue the phenotype of mutant fibroblasts (Fig. 3E)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:22052668:5","pmid":"22052668","section":"animal_model","statement":"Martínez-Glez et al. (2012) noted that the human phenotype differed from that of Bmp1-null mice, which are perinatal lethal without gross skeletal abnormalities, but that, like their patients with umbilical hernias, Bmp1-null mice show consistent herniation of the gut.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Bmp1−/− mice were reported with consistent herniation of the gut and both the proband and her affected brother have large umbilical hernias with intestinal content."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:15542026:1","pmid":"15542026","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Munns et al. (2004) found no type I collagen mutations in 3 unrelated children with a bone fragility disorder associated with an intrinsic mineralization defect.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Type I collagen mutation analysis was negative."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:15542026:2","pmid":"15542026","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Munns et al. (2004) concluded that the disorder was a bone fragility disorder of moderate severity that tends to cause lower extremity fractures and is associated with osteoid accumulation due to an intrinsic mineralization defect. The patients lacked the phenotypic, radiologic, and histomorphometric features of osteogenesis imperfecta, idiopathic juvenile osteoporosis, and mild autosomal dominant osteopetrosis.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["We conclude that this is a bone fragility disorder of moderate severity that tends to cause fractures in the lower extremities and is associated with the accumulation of osteoid due to an intrinsic mineralization defect.","The patients had none of the phenotypic, radiological, or histomorphometric features classically associated with known bone fragility disorders such as osteogenesis imperfecta (OI), idiopathic juvenile osteoporosis (IJO), or mild autosomal dominant osteopetrosis."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:458828:1","pmid":"458828","section":"heterogeneity","statement":"Sillence et al. (1979) studied osteogenesis imperfecta in Victoria, Australia, and identified at least 4 distinct syndromes. The third group had severe progressive deformity of the limbs and spine, and two-thirds had fractures at birth. Their sclerae were less blue than in the first group, and the blueness decreased with age. All cases in this group were sporadic, and the authors suggested that both dominant and recessive genotypes might be responsible.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["confirmed that there are at least four distinct syndromes at present called OI","A third group, two thirds of whom had fractures at birth, showed severe progressive deformity of limbs and spine.","it is likely that the group is heterogeneous with both dominant and recessive genotypes responsible for the syndrome"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["BMP1"],"confirmed":["BMP1"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}